  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/029B8F">Notation <span data-tag="029B8F">5.3.6.1</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ and $\calD $ be $\infty $-categories, and let $\calR $ be a collection of diagrams $\{  \overline{p}_{\alpha }: K_{\alpha }^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \calC \} $. We let $\Fun _{\calR }(\calC , \calD )$ denote the full subcategory of $\Fun (\calC , \calD )$ spanned by those functors which carry each diagram in $\calR $ to a colimit diagram in $\calD $.FunRCD@$\Fun _{\calR }(\calC , \calD )$ </p>
  <p>Let $\calK $ be a collection of simplicial sets. We will say that an $\infty $-category $\calC $ <em>admits $\calK $-indexed colimits</em> if it admits $K$-indexed colimits for each $K \in \calK $. If $f: \calC \rightarrow \calD $ is a functor between $\infty $-categories which admit $\calK $-indexed colimits, then we will say that $f$ <em>preserves $\calK $-indexed colimits</em> if $f$ preserves $K$-indexed colimits, for each $K \in \calK $. We let $\Fun _{\calK }( \calC , \calD )$ denote the full subcategory of $\Fun ( \calC , \calD )$ spanned by those functors which preserves $\calK $-indexed colimits.FunKCD@$\Fun _{\calK }(\calC ,\calD )$ </p>
